  "domain": "Law, courts & regulation",
  "title": "Three contested laws reach the President's desk, and the Tribunal can't settle them",
  "body": "By about **Friday 27 November**, President Karol Nawrocki must act on three of the government's most contested laws. Under Article 122 of the Constitution he has 21 days to sign, veto or refer each one to the Constitutional Tribunal. No decision had been announced as of Monday morning.\n\n**The 'rule of law' act** would void the effect of National Council of the Judiciary (KRS) recommendations made from 2018 to 2026. Judges appointed in that period would return to their earlier posts, and their seats would be re-advertised. The Senate passed it 63–32 on 6 November. PiS calls it a purge and urges a veto; the coalition says it repairs appointments that European courts have found defective.\n\n**Two abortion bills** would restore termination for severe foetal defects and partly decriminalise helping someone obtain an abortion. **The PIT/CIT package** adds a 24% income-tax band, 22% CIT for firms with turnover above €50m and a 5% solidarity levy. It must be decided and published by the end of November to take effect in 2027.\n\nA veto is the likeliest tool for any law the President opposes. Overriding one takes three-fifths of the Sejm, which the coalition does not have. A referral to the Tribunal is murkier, because the Tribunal is itself split. Its general assembly suspended president Bogdan Święczkowski and lifted his immunity. He rejects that decision, and prosecutors may now summon him. Talks at the President's Chancellery on a Tribunal 'reset' based on a PSL plan are boycotted by KO and the Left.\n\n**Why it matters:** the decisions show whether President and government can still pass anything together before 2027. One recent precedent points both ways: on 18 November Nawrocki signed a smaller tax-relief act but vetoed the VAT act. Analysis based on reporting by Interia, Rzeczpospolita/PAP and Finwire.",
